{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793878334,"nonce":"ac47Ggk0lB1bIP2Ig9LbOIQDp1M=","title":"Preserve string ownership transfer independently of collector drop placement","message":"The collector-owned drop policy in 399bcda avoids lifetime/drop placement for plain pool storage and strings. That also skips adopt_into_box, currently coupled to the last-use proof in drop insertion. HIR and CLIF inspection of tokenize shows two $IO$string_adopt_dynamic calls becoming copying box conversions, plus one omitted string free. The hot tostring(i % 100) loop uses immortal small-int strings (0..999), so string_copy returns its source there; the formatted output path can still copy real heap strings.\n\nThis did not account for the measured records/tokenize regressions: those were the new environment key disabling precompiled native snapshots, confirmed by flag-value-zero controls and fixed separately. Nevertheless, ownership transfer is a useful optimization to retain for heap-string-heavy workloads before making collector-managed drops a default.\n\nConsider a cheap, sound unique-use/adoption proof separate from deallocation placement. Preserve all aliases, phi transfers, escape checks and foreign/destructor cleanup; do not restore full lifetime analysis unconditionally and erase the preparation savings. Compare allocation counts and wall time for non-interned strings. Current Lua/Python defaults remain early release.\n\nEvidence: /private/tmp/zyntax-gc-drop-fixed/nuc/tokenize-body.diff, tokenize-interp.diff, tokenize-resume0-outlined-opt.diff, CLIF dumps and analysis.json. Snapshot construction and existing native snapshot releases remain unchanged.","files":null}]}